From 7a7491c968fb0df76498b3889763ce9b2e316a05 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 10:20:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08015/11362] [outer-loop] The daily-prep cron was silently
 destroying a never-prune record, including the leaderboard score history

`scripts/kaggle/prep_daily_submission.py` wrote `ops/arc-daily-prep-status.json` with a
bare `write_text(json.dumps({...six keys}))`. That DELETED the seven submission-trail
keys the file also carried: `submission_ref` (54768046), `submitted_at`,
`submission_status_at_check`, `local_gate_result_at_submit`, the v9 provenance note,
`submitted`, and `prior_submission_scores` -- the actual leaderboard score-by-date
history {"2026-07-15": 0.12, "2026-06-30": 0.08}.

Three things made this worse than an ordinary bug. The script runs from an unattended
systemd timer, so nothing surfaced the loss. The conductor's routine `git add -A` would
have published the deletion as a normal commit. And recovery required knowing to look in
one specific commit (bc2623761) that nothing pointed to. The file on disk is restored.

A plain {**prior, **fresh} merge would have fixed the deletion while introducing a
subtler lie: the previous version's `submitted: true` would sit beside a brand-new,
unsubmitted kernel version and read as though THIS prep had already been submitted. So
on a version change the submission fields are retired into an append-only
`submission_history` -- kept, per never-prune -- and cleared from the live record, while
`prior_submission_scores` stays live because it is cumulative, not per-version.

The logic was unreachable by any test while buried inside a `main()` that shells out to
Kaggle, so it is extracted to a pure `_merge_prep_status`. The 11 tests are written
against the actual destroyed record rather than a synthetic happy path, per the
project's regression-test discipline: that no prior key is EVER dropped without being
archived, that the score history survives, that a fresh version is not left looking
already-submitted, that re-prepping the SAME version keeps its own submission fields,
and that history accumulates across successive version changes rather than overwriting.

Generalised as REQ-HARNESS-6040: any unattended writer of a durable never-prune record
must merge rather than replace, must retire superseded per-version fields into an
append-only history instead of deleting them, must keep cumulative fields live, and must
expose the merge as a pure function so it is testable without the writer's side effects.

Two pre-existing UP017s and a whole-file reformat came along because touching the file
brought it into ruff's scope for the first time; both are the project's own tooling, and
the now-dead `timezone` import they left behind is removed.

